Remote access
administrator-05-remote-access-and-the-machine
The page has six views, chosen with the keys 1 to 6. The doors view shows what REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH say with no keyword, which is what the machine reports after reading sd.conf and the firewall rules back. A changes the API (on, local or off): after its box the terminal is given to REMOTE.API, which asks whether to restart SD when the listener moves to or from off, because SD opens the socket only when it starts, and a restart ends every session, this one too. S changes ssh (on or off) at once. Turning either on lets other computers reach this one, so its box wants the word OPEN typed. The routes view shows each user account with the doors it has and how it can be reached; the switches themselves are on the gates page.
The servers view lists the SDNet servers of the netservers file: the name, the address, the remote account and mode, the pin shortened, and the local accounts that may use the server. X deletes the one under the cursor after a box. N asks for every field, checks them as SET.SERVER does, and gives the terminal to the verb, whose CONNECT step shows the key the server presents and asks whether to trust it. A local account also needs the NET gate.
The connected view lists every session by the door it came through, and the TLS view shows this machine's certificate and the hash of its public key, which another SD server pins. The remote admin view shows what REMOTE.SDSYS says with no keyword: whether the remote administration account SDRADMIN may sign in over ssh, one line for each wall. O opens it after a box that wants the word OPEN typed, giving the terminal to REMOTE.SDSYS ON, which makes the account and asks its password the first time; F shuts it again and logs SDRADMIN out. Each command shows what it does and runs only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. F5 reads everything again.
